questions.prompt:
A junior engineer wrote this Node TCP server for a protocol where clients send JSON messages. It works in local testing but in production it occasionally throws `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input` or misses messages. What's wrong, and how would you fix it?

```js
const net = require("net");
net.createServer(socket => {
  socket.on("data", chunk => {
    const msg = JSON.parse(chunk.toString());
    handle(msg);
  });
}).listen(9000);
```

questions.answer:
TCP has no message boundaries, so a `data` chunk may hold half a message or two messages. Use framing, e.g. newline-delimited JSON, and buffer:

```js
net.createServer(socket => {
  let buf = "";
  socket.on("data", chunk => {
    buf += chunk.toString("utf8");
    let i;
    while ((i = buf.indexOf("\n")) !== -1) {
      const line = buf.slice(0, i);
      buf = buf.slice(i + 1);
      if (line) handle(JSON.parse(line));
    }
  });
}).listen(9000);
```
(Clients must end each message with `\n`. A length prefix works too. Using `socket.setEncoding('utf8')` also avoids splitting multi-byte characters.)
